main.cpp
--cpp17 --object-bits 12
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ factory returns derived through base unique_ptr: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
--
Currently: "invalid implicit conversion from 'struct unique_ptr' to
'struct unique_ptr'" on the derived-typed return.
Round-87 diagnosis (56-line header-free kernel reproduces; committed
here as the libc++ carrier): the switch is IRRELEVANT -- a single
`return uptr<const Derived>(...)` from a `uptr<const Base>` function
fails identically, while the same conversion in DIRECT-INITIALIZATION
works.  Root: cpp_typecheckt::typecheck_return materialises the result
object through a constructor call only when the return type has a
DESTRUCTOR, and skips operands that are already temporaries; a
converting temporary of a different class type therefore falls through
to implicit_typecast, which knows no user-defined conversion
([stmt.return]/2 + [dcl.init.general]/16.6.1 require considering the
CONVERTING constructors, [unique.ptr.single.ctor]/26).
ROUND-88 UPDATE: the front-end defect IS FIXED (see CORE
cpp17_converting_return_move_ctor, a header-free carrier of exactly
this shape, and the commit "converting constructors for class-typed
return operands").  What remains in THIS test is a DEEPER layer inside
libstdc++'s unique_ptr: with the conversion now performed, symbolic
execution aborts on symex_assign's invariant (lhs.type() == rhs.type())
assigning a raw `pointer` to a
`unique_ptr<const timestampert, default_delete<...>>` -- i.e. the
converting constructor's own instantiation (the __uniq_ptr_data /
tuple-of-pointer internals) produces a mistyped member assignment.
That internals layer is the remaining work; the test is kept as its
carrier.
